## How to attribute

Attribution is the point of choosing CC BY over CC0. This benchmark's value is
that it gets cited, and the attribution requirement is what makes citation the
default rather than an afterthought. A sufficient credit line:

> RiverAlpha AI market-prediction benchmark, by Park jin young.
> https://github.com/4dbq9mznvp-ui/riveralpha-data — CC BY 4.0.
> Retrieved <date>, covering rounds through <roundId>.

Naming the round range matters more than it looks. The log is append-only and
grows daily, so "the RiverAlpha data" is not a fixed object; a citation without
a boundary cannot be checked against the chain hash it was drawn from.

If you modify the data, say so. CC BY 4.0 requires indicating changes, and for a
benchmark whose whole claim is that records were committed before outcomes, an
unmarked modified copy is actively misleading.

